TAR(1)                                       RETRO UNIX Z80

NAME
     tar, tap - file archives on the cassette

SYNOPSIS
     tar cvf /dev/cassette/name file ...
     tar tvf /dev/cassette/name [file ...]
     tar xvf /dev/cassette/name [file ...]
     tap r file ...    tap t    tap x [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
     tar writes files to the cassette as one archive and reads
     them back. A directory is archived with everything below
     it. Each file keeps its full path name, mode, size and
     modification time; packed files go to tape packed and come
     back packed.

     c    create an archive (start the recorder first)
     t    list an archive
     x    extract an archive, or only the files named, and the
          files below a directory named
     v    verbose: kind (- file, d directory, z packed), size
          and path of every file
     f    the archive: /dev/cassette/name. The name, up to 10
          characters, labels the archive on the tape, so one
          tape can hold several; t and x wind on to the archive
          with that name. /dev/cassette alone means the next
          archive.

     Extracting replaces existing files without asking (it says
     "replaced") and makes the directories a path needs. A file
     that does not fit on the disk is removed again.

     tap is UNIX v1's name for the same archiver: tap r, t and
     x are tar c, t and x on the next archive, verbose.

THE TAPE
     Standard Spectrum blocks: a header named after the
     archive, then for every file a header named after it and
     its data in blocks of up to 512 bytes, then an empty
     header. Tape browsers show the names. Devices and the
     resident programs in /bin are not archived.

EXAMPLES
     tar cvf /dev/cassette/clive /usr/clive
     tar tvf /dev/cassette/clive
     tar xvf /dev/cassette/clive /usr/clive/notes
